      "source": "Datasheet Rev. L Theory of Operation"
    }
  ],
  "reputation": {
    "summary": "A mid-2000s Head-Fi and CMoy favourite, not a top-tier legend. Tangent found it 'not aggressive, but not laid-back', 'a bit veiled', and a good battery pick alongside AD823 and AD8610. Head-Fi users heard it as delicate, refined and clear with smooth highs; one found it too mellow. No audio-band THD+N measurements (ASR, Groner) exist.",
    "sonic_descriptors": [
      "between AD8610 and OPA227",
      "a bit veiled",
      "delicate yet refined",
      "clear, smooth highs",
      "clear, focused, cool, fast",
      "really wide soundstage",
      "not harsh or tiring",
      "too mellow (dissenting)"
    ],
    "typical_uses": [
      "CMoy / PIMETA battery headphone amps (low minimum supply)",
      "Rolling on SOIC-to-DIP adapters at ±12 V or less",
      "Line and headphone gain stages and buffers",
      "Photodiode preamps, ADC drivers, video buffers, instrument front ends"
    ],
    "caveats": [
      "24 V total maximum (26.4 V absolute): never fit in ±15 V or ±18 V sockets.",
      "SMD only; a 145 MHz part on an adapter can oscillate. Decouple at the pins; isolate loads over 20–25 pF.",
      "JFET range ends 2.6–3.3 V below +Vs, then Ib jumps to tens of µA. Differential input limited to 1.8 V.",
      "High 1/f corner: about 11 nV/√Hz at 1 kHz, 33 at 100 Hz, worse than the 7 nV headline.",
      "6.4 mA per amp; output shorts 'will likely cause catastrophic failure'. Sonic reports (2003–2010) disagree."
    ],
